.Dd $Mdocdate: August 2 2026 $
.Dt FuguLib::Crypto 3p
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm FuguLib::Crypto
.Nd randomness and cryptographic primitives
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal
use FuguLib::Crypto;

my $nonce = FuguLib::Crypto->random_bytes(12);
my $password = FuguLib::Crypto->random_password(32);

my ($secret, $public) = FuguLib::Crypto->ed25519_keypair;
my $signature = FuguLib::Crypto->ed25519_sign($msg, $secret, $public);

FuguLib::Crypto->preload;    # before a pledge without prot_exec
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
gives the randomness and the primitives that a HomeKit-class protocol
needs: Ed25519 signatures, X25519 key agreement, HKDF over SHA-512,
and ChaCha20-Poly1305.
.Pp
Every method is a class method.
The module keeps no state and never logs.
A programming error dies.
There is no partial result and no quiet fallback, because a caller
cannot recover from a key that is not a key.
.Pp
.Fn random_bytes
and
.Fn random_password
use core Perl only.
Each other group loads its
.Vt Crypt::*
library on first use, so a program that never signs anything never
needs that library installed.
.Ss random_bytes
.Fn random_bytes "$length"
returns
.Fa $length
bytes from
.Pa /dev/urandom .
.Pp
The method dies when the device does not open, and dies on a short
read.
Neither one has a recovery: a caller that continues with fewer bytes
than it asked for builds a key with a known prefix.
.Ss random_password
.Fn random_password "$length"
returns a random password of exactly
.Fa $length
characters.
The default length is 32.
.Pp
The alphabet is URL-safe base64, so the password survives a shell, a
URL and a configuration file with no quoting.
.Ss preload
.Fn preload
loads every
.Vt Crypt::*
library now.
.Pp
A daemon that pledges without
.Cm prot_exec
must call this before it pledges.
A lazy
.Ic require
after the pledge opens a shared object, and that kills the process.
The method dies when a library is missing, because a daemon that finds
that out after the pledge cannot report it.
.Ss ed25519_keypair
.Fn ed25519_keypair
returns
.Pq Fa $secret_key , Fa $public_key
for a fresh identity.
.Ss ed25519_sign
.Fn ed25519_sign "$message" "$secret_key" "$public_key"
returns the 64-byte signature over the message.
.Ss ed25519_verify
.Fn ed25519_verify "$signature" "$message" "$public_key"
reports if the signature is valid for the message and the key.
.Ss x25519_keypair
.Fn x25519_keypair
returns
.Pq Fa $secret_key , Fa $public_key
for a fresh exchange.
.Ss x25519_shared_secret
.Fn x25519_shared_secret "$our_secret" "$their_public"
returns the 32-byte shared secret.
Both sides of an exchange derive the same value.
.Ss hkdf_sha512
.Fn hkdf_sha512 "$ikm" "$salt" "$info" "$length"
derives
.Fa $length
bytes from the input keying material, with HKDF over SHA-512.
The derivation is deterministic.
.Ss chacha20poly1305_encrypt
.Fn chacha20poly1305_encrypt "$key" "$nonce" "$plaintext" "$aad"
returns
.Pq Fa $ciphertext , Fa $tag .
.Fa $aad
is the additional data, which the tag authenticates and the cipher
does not hide.
Its default is the empty string.
.Ss chacha20poly1305_decrypt
.Fn chacha20poly1305_decrypt "$key" "$nonce" "$ciphertext" "$tag" "$aad"
returns the plaintext, or
.Dv undef
when the tag does not verify.
A failed verification is the normal answer for a forged or damaged
message, so it is not fatal.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn random_bytes
and
.Fn random_password
return a string of the asked-for length.
.Pp
.Fn preload
returns the number of libraries it loaded.
A second call returns 0.
.Pp
.Fn ed25519_keypair
and
.Fn x25519_keypair
return a two-element list.
.Fn ed25519_verify
returns true or false.
.Fn chacha20poly1305_encrypt
returns a two-element list.
.Fn chacha20poly1305_decrypt
returns the plaintext, or
.Dv undef .
The other methods return a string.
.Sh EXAMPLES
This example is the startup order of a daemon that pledges:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# Load every shared object while the library tree is reachable
FuguLib::Crypto->preload;

FuguLib::Sandbox->unveil(paths => \e@paths);
FuguLib::Sandbox->unveil_lock;

# The promise set has no prot_exec, so nothing may load after here
FuguLib::Sandbox->pledge(promises => 'stdio rpath wpath inet');
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn random_bytes
dies when
.Pa /dev/urandom
does not open, on a short read, and on a length that is not positive.
.Pp
Every method that needs an optional library dies when that library is
missing.
The message names the module and the group of functions that needed
it, because
.Dq Can't locate Crypt/Ed25519.pm in @INC
tells an operator nothing about which feature stopped working.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr arc4random 3 ,
.Xr FuguLib::Sandbox 3p ,
.Xr random 4
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Dick Olsson Aq Mt hi@senzilla.io
.Sh CAVEATS
The module does not choose a nonce and does not count one up.
A nonce that repeats under one key destroys the security of
ChaCha20-Poly1305.
The protocol that uses the cipher owns that rule, because only the
protocol knows what a message is.
.Pp
.Fn random_password
trims a base64 string to the asked-for length.
A password of
.Fa n
characters therefore carries about six bits of entropy for each
character, and not eight.
